import "core/primitives.ct"
import "core/data.ct"


module(Data/Algebra)
  # # Equivalence and Equality
    This file defines equivalence relations ([Eqv]), the [Eq] class of types that have a canonical
    equality, and the [operator==] / [operator!=] built on top of them. It is the foundation that
    ordering ([Ord], in [Data/Algebra/ord]) builds upon.
  # ## Equivalence Relations
    An [Eqv] is the class of equivalence relations. Each object in the class is a single
    equivalence relation over some type, and supplies the [rel] that decides whether two values of
    that type are to be considered equivalent.
    TODO The type an [Eqv] relates is not data, so it cannot be a type parameter of the class the
    way the element type of a [List] is. Once refinement types can state it, write it as what each
    relation covers and drop the [#invalid] below:
    ```
    :Eqv.over -> TypeSet
    :Eqv.rel[$T](a -> $T, b -> $T) | this.over.superset($T) -> Boolean
    :$T.eq[$T -> /Data/Algebra/Eq] -> /Data/Algebra/Eqv | $out.over.superset($T)
    ```
  class(Eqv)

  :Eqv.rel[$T](a -> $T, b -> $T) -> Boolean
    # [rel] is the equivalence function of an [Eqv]. It is a class declaration: every object in the
      class defines it for the type it relates, and it holds only for [a] and [b] of that type —
      the type the object's own definitions of [rel] are written over. See the per-type instances
      (such as [BoolEqv]) and [eq], which names the one for an [Eq] type.
    #invalid

  # ## The Eq class
    The [Eq] class is used to define values which can be compared for equality. While naive tuple
    equality often works, some types have equality that behaves differently and must be defined.
    For example, a TreeSet can insert items in different order and produce different trees, but with
    equal sets. An [Eq] type names its canonical equivalence with [eq], from which [equal],
    [operator==] and [operator!=] follow.
  class(Eq)

  # [eq] is the standard equivalence relation for an [Eq] type; [equal] is the single method
  #   through which [operator==] dispatches.
  :$T.eq[$T -> /Data/Algebra/Eq] -> /Data/Algebra/Eqv

  :$T.equal[$T -> /Data/Algebra/Eq](other -> $T) -> Boolean

  # [operator==] is the equality operator; [operator!=] is its negation. Creating an instance of
  #   [Eq] only requires defining [eq]; [equal], [operator==] and [operator!=] then follow.
  (_ :: $T == _ :: $T)[$T -> /Data/Algebra/Eq] -> Boolean

  (_ :: $T != _ :: $T)[$T -> /Data/Algebra/Eq] -> Boolean

  # ## Example and law objects
    The example and law tests reference named [Eqv] objects, since the class is defined by its
    members rather than constructed inline. [IntEqExample] is ordinary integer equality, and
    [AllEqv] relates every pair (handy for demonstrating the symmetry and transitivity laws on
    distinct values).
  data(IntEqExample, isa= [/Data/Algebra/Eqv])

  :IntEqExample.rel(a -> Integer, b -> Integer)= /Data/Primitive/intEq(l= a, r= b)

  data(AllEqv, isa= [/Data/Algebra/Eqv])

  :AllEqv.rel(a, b)= True

  # ## Example
    The simplest [Eqv] over [Integer] compares its values with the built-in integer equality;
    [AllEqv] relates every pair.
  > IntEqExample.rel(a= 2, b= 2)
    >>> True
  > IntEqExample.rel(a= 2, b= 3)
    >>> False
  > AllEqv.rel(a= 2, b= 3)
    >>> True
  # ## Laws of equivalence
    Reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity are properties of the [Eqv] class itself: every
    equivalence relation must satisfy them, so each is a property test checked against every [Eqv]
    instance (here [$E]) at arbitrary members of the type that instance relates (here [$T]).
    Symmetry is written as a biconditional to make it non-vacuous, and transitivity as a material
    implication, so ordinary equalities satisfy it vacuously and a relation like [AllEqv] exercises
    the premise.
    TODO The laws are set aside while [rel] is [#invalid]: they read the type an instance relates
    from a parameter of [Eqv], which it no longer has, so they cannot generate values the instance
    is defined over. Restore them once [Eqv.over] can be stated:
    ```
    testEqvReflexive[$E -> Eqv[$T]](e -> $E, t -> $T) =
      #test
      #assert(test= e.rel(a= t, b= t))
      0
    testEqvSymmetric[$E -> Eqv[$T]](e -> $E, x -> $T, y -> $T) =
      #test
      #assert(test= e.rel(a= x, b= y) == e.rel(a= y, b= x))
      0
    testEqvTransitive[$E -> Eqv[$T]](e -> $E, x -> $T, y -> $T, z -> $T) =
      #test
      #assert(test= ~(e.rel(a= x, b= y) && e.rel(a= y, b= z)) || e.rel(a= x, b= z))
      0
    ```
  # The per-type [Eqv] instances ([BoolEqv], [IntOrdering], [StrEqv], …) and each type's [eq] /
  #   [equal] live alongside their types in [Data/bool], [Data/num], [Data/char] and [Data/string]; this file
  #   defines only the abstract [Eqv]/[Eq] classes and the [operator==] / [operator!=] built on them.
  (l :: $T == r :: $T)[$T -> /Data/Algebra/Eq]= l.equal(other= r)

  (l :: $T != r :: $T)[$T -> /Data/Algebra/Eq]= ~(l == r)
